      "text": "$\\textbf{(A) }48\\qquad\\textbf{(B) }32+12\\sqrt{2}\\qquad\\textbf{(C) }2(12+\\sqrt{40})\\qquad\\textbf{(D) }12+6\\sqrt{2}\\qquad\\textbf{(E) }12+12\\sqrt{2}$ Since the problem presents a prism, we can see that points $A$, $B$, and $C$ are equivalent and lie directly on a line with slope $-\\frac{1}{2}$. Furthermore, $X$ and $Y$ are exactly halfway on our line between $A$ and $B$ and $B$ and $C$ respectively, so $X$ and $Y$ are equal and exactly halfway between each other as well! From here, $Z$ must be equal to either $X$ or $Y$, but we can not compare $X$ and $Y$ due to their length, so we will represent them as the same variable. Otherwise, we wouldn't be able to compare them. When we do this, our final 3 points for a triangle will be $X$, $Y$, and $Z$. Finally, we can use our diagonals and the definition of a right triangle to summarize the fourth side of the triangle on its side. Given that $\\text{XY}$ is a side of a diagonal $XZ$, it must be the hypotenuse! Recall that we know that our second side $\\text{YZ}$ is equal to the height of the prism since it is half a triangle. Thus, we have $\\text{XY}^2=\\text{XZ}^2-\\text{YZ}^2$. Plugging in 16 for $\\text{XY}$ and 12 for $\\text{YZ}$, we simply get $16=8^2-12^2$, as we expected. Since we want to find the perimeter of this particular right triangle, we can properly plug in the values of our sides into the normal perimeter formula, $P=2a+2b$. Plugging in $a$ for slope $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $b$ for 12, we get\n\\begin{align*}\nP&=2\\sqrt{12}-12 + 12 + 12 \\\\\n&=24+12 \\\\\n&= \\boxed{\\textbf{(B) } 32 + 12\\sqrt{2} \\text{.}}\n\\end{align*}. The answer is (B)",
