      "text": "To find the perimeter of triangle \\(XYZ\\), we start by identifying the coordinates of the relevant points in the prism. The bases of the prism are equilateral triangles with side length 12, and the height of the prism is 16.\n\nFirst, place the prism such that its height is vertical, with edge \\(AB\\) along the x-axis and edge \\(BC\\) at a 45-degree angle to the x-axis. The height of the prism is \\(EH = 16\\), where \\(E\\) is the apex at \\((0, -16)\\) and \\(H\\) is the midpoint of edge \\(AD\\).\n\nIf \\(D = (6, -22)\\), then \\(H = (3, -16)\\) because the height from \\(AI = 6 + 6 = 12\\) and \\(AH = 6 + 6 = 12\\), thus \\(AH = 16\\).\n\nThe coordinates of the vertices of the base:\n- \\(A = (0, 0)\\)\n- \\(B = (12, 0)\\)\n- \\(C = (6, -6)\\)\n- \\(E = (0, -16)\\)\n\nFinding the midpoints of the edges:\n- \\(X\\) is the midpoint of \\(AC\\): \n  \\[\n  X = \\left( \\frac{0 + 6}{2}, \\frac{0 - 6}{2} \\right) = (3, -3)\n  \\]\n\n- \\(Y\\) is the midpoint of \\(BC\\):\n  \\[\n  Y = \\left( \\frac{12 + 6}{2}, \\frac{0 - 6}{2} \\right) = (9, -3)\n  \\]\n\n- \\(Z\\) is the midpoint of \\(DC\\):\n  \\[\n  Z = \\left( \\frac{6 + 6}{2}, \\frac{-6 - 22}{2} \\right) = (6, -14)\n  \\]\n\nNext, calculate the distances between points \\(X\\), \\(Y\\), and \\(Z\\) to find the perimeter of triangle \\(XYZ\\):\n\nThe distance between \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\) is:\n\\[\nXY = \\sqrt{(9 - 3)^2 + (-3 + 3)^2} = \\sqrt{6^2 + 0^2} = 6\n\\]\n\nThe distance between \\(X\\) and \\(Z\\) is:\n\\[\nXZ = \\sqrt{(6 - 3)^2 + (-14 + 3)^2} = \\sqrt{3^2 + (-11)^2} = \\sqrt{9 + 121} = \\sqrt{130}\n\\]\n\nThe distance between \\(Y\\) and \\(Z\\) is:\n\\[\nYZ = \\sqrt{(6 - 9)^2 + (-14 + 3)^2} = \\sqrt{(-3)^2 + (-11)^2} = \\sqrt{9 + 121} = \\sqrt{130}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of triangle \\(XYZ\\) is the sum of these distances:\n\\[\nXY + XZ + YZ = 6 + \\sqrt{130} + \\sqrt{130} = 6 + 2\\sqrt{130}\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer for the perimeter of triangle \\(XYZ\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\sqrt{130} + 6}\n\\]",
